   "text": "Portable power station vs. generator for home backup: which should you buy? | Roof & Router Skip to content Home tech \n Portable power station vs. generator for home backup: which should you buy?\n By Roof & Router Team . Updated October 12, 2026.\n Photo: Unsplash Short answer: Choose a portable power station for short outages and indoor essentials like the fridge, Wi-Fi, phones and lights, because it is silent and gives off no fumes. Choose a generator for multi-day outages and heavy loads like well pumps and air conditioners, because it runs as long as you have fuel. Run a generator only outdoors, at least 20 feet from the house.\n Skip to what to buy (10 types) ↓ \n The short version\n Portable power station \n Portable generator \n Energy source \n Battery, recharged from the wall, solar or a car \n Gasoline, propane or both \n How long it runs \n Until the battery is empty (hours to about a day for essentials) \n As long as you keep adding fuel \n Typical continuous output \n About 300 to 4,000 W (some expandable models more) \n About 1,000 to 12,000+ W \n Indoor use \n Yes \n Never. Outdoors only, 20+ feet from the house \n Noise \n Near silent (small cooling fans) \n Loud; inverter models are quieter \n Upkeep \n Charge it every few months \n Oil changes, fresh fuel, regular test runs \n Best for \n Short outages, apartments, essentials, medical devices \n Long outages, pumps, AC, furnaces, whole-house loads \n How long will a power station run a fridge?\n A portable power station is a large lithium battery with an inverter that turns its stored energy into normal 120-volt outlet power. Two numbers matter:\n Capacity (Wh): how much energy it holds. This sets how long things run.\n Output (W): how much power it can give at once. This sets what can run together.\n The limit is that the energy is fixed. A 2,000 Wh unit holds 2,000 Wh, and about 85% of that reaches your devices after the inverter’s losses. A full-size fridge uses roughly 1 to 2 kWh a day, so a 2,000 Wh station keeps a fridge alone running for roughly one to one and a half days, less if anything else is plugged in.\n Recharging extends it: from a wall outlet when power returns, from a car’s 12 V socket (slowly), or from solar panels.\n How much solar adds: a rough estimate is panel watts × hours of full sun × 0.75 for real-world losses. A 200 W panel with 4 hours of full sun gives about 200 × 4 × 0.75 = 600 Wh in a day. Winter days, clouds and shade give less.\n Which is better for long power outages?\n A generator burns fuel to spin an alternator. It produces power for as long as it has fuel, which is why it wins for long outages. Most portable generators run gasoline; dual-fuel models also run on propane, which stores for years without going stale. Store propane tanks outdoors, never in a garage or basement, and expect somewhat less power output when running on propane.\n The limits are noise, upkeep and, above all, carbon monoxide. The U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission says an average of nearly 100 people in the U.S. die each year from carbon monoxide poisoning from portable generators (2017 to 2019 average). Generator exhaust can kill in minutes.\n CPSC safety rules:\n Never run a generator inside a home, garage, basement, crawlspace or shed, even with doors and windows open.\n Run it outside only, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents.\n Look for models with an automatic CO shut-off feature.\n Install battery-powered or battery-backup CO alarms inside the home.\n Let the generator cool down before refueling, and do not run it in rain or snow without a cover made for that purpose.\n Inverter generators produce cleaner power that is safe for laptops and other electronics, and they are quieter and more fuel-efficient at light loads. Conventional (open-frame) generators give more watts for the money but are louder.\n Which one for your situation?\n Situation \n Better pick \n Why \n Outages of a few hours, a few times a year \n Power station \n It sits ready, needs no fuel and runs in the kitchen \n Apartment or condo \n Power station \n There is likely nowhere safe to run a generator 20 feet from the building \n CPAP or other medical device \n Power station \n Safer indoors. Turn off a CPAP’s heated humidifier, or use the maker’s DC adapter, to stretch the battery \n Outages that last days \n Generator, or a station plus solar \n Only fuel or daily recharging keeps up \n Well pump, sump pump or window AC \n Usually a generator \n Motors need a big starting burst, and long runs drain a battery. Large stations (3,000 W+) can run some briefly \n Gas furnace \n Either \n The blower draws a few hundred watts, but it is usually hardwired, so you need an electrician-installed inlet or transfer switch \n Space heater \n Generator, or skip it \n A 1,500 W heater drains a 2,000 Wh station in about an hour \n Mix of quiet nights and heavy daytime loads \n Both \n Run the station indoors overnight and the generator outdoors by day to cover heavy loads and recharge the station \n For oxygen concentrators or ventilators, talk to your doctor and ask your utility about its medical priority program.\n The costly mistake is buying for the wrong outage. A station bought for multi-day storms leaves you dark by day two without solar. A generator bought for an apartment has nowhere safe to run.\n Is a generator or power station cheaper to run?\n Power station: recharging from the grid costs very little. A full 2,000 Wh charge is about 2.3 kWh from the wall, around 40 cents at 18 cents per kWh. The bigger cost is the battery wearing out over time. Many current models use lithium iron phosphate (LiFePO4) cells and list ratings of several thousand charge cycles, which is many years of occasional outage use.\n Generator: the cost is fuel and maintenance. Fuel use depends on the size of the generator and how hard it is working; check the run time at 25% and 50% load on the spec sheet. Gasoline goes stale in storage, so use fuel stabilizer and rotate fuel, or choose a dual-fuel model and store propane.\n How to connect a generator or power station to your house safely\n The simple way is extension cords from the unit to the devices you need. Use outdoor-rated cords heavy enough for the load; a cord that feels warm is too thin.\n To power circuits in your home’s wiring, such as a well pump or furnace that is hardwired, you need a transfer switch or interlock kit installed by a licensed electrician . Never plug a generator or power station into a wall outlet to “backfeed” the house. It violates electrical codes, can start a fire, and can send power back down the line to kill utility workers. Some power station makers sell home backup kits that work with a professionally installed transfer switch.\n How to size either one\n List what you need to run during an outage.\n Add up running watts for everything that will run at the same time. That is the continuous output you need.\n Find the device with the largest start-up surge, usually the fridge or a pump, and add that surge to the total. That is the surge output you need.\n For a power station, also add up watt-hours per day to size the battery.\n The calculator below does steps 2 to 4 for a power station, using typical watts you can adjust. For a generator, use the generator size calculator , which keeps the load under 90% of capacity, as Home Depot advises.\n 1. What do you want to keep running?\n Watts are typical values. For the exact number, check the label on the device or its manual. Hours means hours the device actually draws power in a day.\n Device Running W Starting W Hours/day Qty \n Device name Running W Starting W Hours/day Qty Remove \n Device name Running W Starting W Hours/day Qty Remove \n Device name Running W Starting W Hours/day Qty Remove \n Device name Running W Starting W Hours/day Qty Remove \n Common devices Refrigerator (full size) Chest freezer Furnace blower (gas furnace, 1/3 hp) Sump pump (1/3 hp) Well pump (1/2 hp) Window AC (8,000 BTU) Space heater Electric blanket Microwave (1,000 W cooking power) Coffee maker TV (55-inch LED) Laptop Wi-Fi router and modem Phone charging LED light bulb Box fan Add device Add your own \n Days of backup 1 day 2 days 3 days Usable share of the battery (%) \n 2. What you need\n Battery capacity 2,065 Wh Look for 3,000 Wh or more.\n Continuous output 300 W Everything on your list at once is 189 W. This adds 20% headroom.\n Surge output 1,239 W Running load plus the biggest start-up jump ( 1,050 W).\n Already have one, or looking at a model? Its capacity in Wh A 1,000 Wh unit runs this list for about 12 hours.\n How this is calculated Daily energy = sum of (running watts × hours per day × quantity) = 1,755 Wh.\n Battery capacity = daily energy × days ÷ usable share ( 85 %). Part of every battery's rated capacity is lost in the inverter that turns battery power into wall-outlet power.\n Continuous output = everything running at once × 1.2, rounded up to the next 100 W.\n Surge output assumes motors (fridge, pumps, furnace fan) start one at a time, so only the largest start-up jump is added.\n What to buy\n The main types, who each one suits, and what to check before you buy. Each link opens matching products at that store.\n Portable power station (1,000-2,000Wh)\n Best for: short outages, apartments and indoor essentials such as a fridge, Wi-Fi, phones, lights and a CPAP.\n Look for: continuous and surge watts that cover your fridge, UL 2743 certification, and a LiFePO4 battery with a high cycle rating.\n See on Amazon ↗ Also at Walmart · Home Depot · Lowe's · Best Buy \n Large power station (3,000 W+ output)\n Best for: short runs of a sump pump, well pump or window AC, which smaller stations cannot start.\n Look for: a surge rating above your motor's starting watts, and expansion batteries if you need more runtime.\n See on Amazon ↗ Also at Walmart · Home Depot · Lowe's · Best Buy \n Inverter generator\n Best for: laptops and other electronics during long outages. Quieter and more fuel-efficient at light loads.\n Look for: running watts that cover your list, and an automatic CO shut-off, as CPSC recommends.\n See on Amazon ↗ Also at Walmart · Home Depot · Lowe's · Best Buy \n Dual fuel portable generator\n Best for: multi-day outages. Propane stores for years without going stale.\n Look for: the watts listed for propane, which are usually lower than for gasoline, and a CO shut-off.\n See on Amazon ↗ Also at Walmart · Home Depot · Lowe's · Best Buy \n Portable solar panel (100-200 W)\n Best for: refilling a power station each day. A 200 W panel adds about 600 Wh with 4 hours of full sun.\n Look for: a connector that fits your station and panel watts within its maximum solar input.\n See on Amazon ↗ Also at Walmart · Home Depot · Lowe's · Best Buy \n Generator transfer switch or interlock kit\n Best for: powering hardwired loads such as a furnace or well pump without backfeeding.\n Look for: a model that matches your panel. A licensed electrician installs it.\n See on Amazon ↗ Also at Walmart · Home Depot · Lowe's · Best Buy \n Heavy-duty outdoor extension cord\n Best for: running devices straight from a generator outside or a station in another room.\n Look for: an outdoor-rated cord heavy enough for the load. A cord that feels warm is too thin.\n See on Amazon ↗ Also at Walmart · Home Depot · Lowe's · Best Buy \n Battery carbon monoxide alarm\n Best for: every home that runs a generator, as CPSC advises.\n Look for: battery power or battery backup, so it works when the grid is down.\n See on Amazon ↗ Also at Walmart · Home Depot · Lowe's · Best Buy \n Fuel stabilizer\n Best for: keeping stored gasoline usable between outages.\n Look for: a stabilizer made for small engines, added when you fill the can.\n See on Amazon ↗ Also at Walmart · Home Depot · Lowe's · Best Buy \n Generator running cover\n Best for: running a generator in rain or snow, which CPSC says to do only with a cover made for that purpose.\n Look for: a cover designed for use while the generator runs, sized for your model.\n See on Amazon ↗ Also at Walmart · Home Depot · Lowe's · Best Buy \n These links open search results at Amazon, Walmart, The Home Depot, Lowe's and Best Buy. We do not earn anything from them, and no brand paid to be listed.\n Quick answers\n Can I run a power station indoors? Yes. It produces no exhaust. Keep it ventilated and away from water while charging, follow the maker's instructions, and choose one certified to the UL 2743 safety standard. Stop using any unit that is damaged or swollen. \n Which is better for long power outages, a power station or a generator? A generator, because it runs as long as you add fuel. A power station only lasts until its battery is empty, unless you can refill it each day from solar panels or a generator. \n How long will a power station run a fridge? About 85% of a station's capacity reaches your devices. A full-size fridge uses roughly 1 to 2 kWh a day, so a 2,000 Wh station runs a fridge alone for roughly one to one and a half days. \n Can a power station run a whole house? Not a portable one. Whole-home backup needs an installed home battery system or a standby generator, sized and installed by a professional. \n Is a power station worth it instead of a generator? For short outages and essentials, often yes: no fuel, no fumes, no noise and little upkeep. For long outages or heavy loads, a generator is still the more practical source of large amounts of energy. \n Can I charge a power station from a generator? Yes. Run the generator outside for a few hours to charge the station, then use the station quietly indoors overnight. \n Sources\n U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission: Winter storm CO and generator safety (2024) \n U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission: What to know about portable generators \n Researched and written by the Roof & Router Team \n We work out home, yard and home tech questions from manufacturer specs, government data and university extension research, and we show the math on every page.\n We do not test products in a lab. Read how we research to see where our numbers come from.\n Related guides\n Home tech\n What size generator do I need for my house?\n Home tech\n What can a 1,000Wh power station run, and for how long?\n Home\n How to prepare your home for a winter power outage",
