   "text": "Skip to content\n\nTested Watts\n\nReviews\nGuides\nSolar & Charging\nCalculator\nAbout\nSearch\nFind your size\n\nHome » Guides\n\nSolar Generator vs Gas Generator: Costs, Safety and Which to Buy\nAR\n\nAlejandro Rodríguez, MD\nPhysician (MD, Colombia)\n\nSep 23, 2026\nSep 25, 2026\n\nSpecs verified, runtimes calculated. We don’t lab-test power stations (yet). How we research\n\nQuick answer:\na gas generator gives you more power for less money and can run for days as long as you have fuel, but it’s loud, needs maintenance and must run outdoors because of carbon monoxide. A solar generator (a portable power station plus solar panels) is silent, safe to use indoors and needs no fuel, but it stores a limited amount of energy and recharges slowly on cloudy days. For a refrigerator, a CPAP and electronics in a house or apartment, solar is usually the better fit. For long outages with big loads like a well pump, gas still wins.\n\nPrices and fuel figures in this article are approximate ranges and clearly labeled assumptions, not quotes. Check current prices before you buy. See How We Research.\n\nOn this page\n\nThe comparison at a glance\nSafety: the difference that matters most\nCost example: a 3-day power outage\nWhen a gas generator is the better choice\nWhen a solar generator is the better choice\nThe hybrid option many people choose\nFrequently asked questions\nThe bottom line\nThe comparison at a glance\n\tGas generator (portable inverter type)\tSolar generator\nTypical upfront cost\tLower for the same output\tHigher, especially for large capacity\nRunning cost\tFuel, oil and maintenance\tClose to zero\nPower output\tHigh; can handle big motors\tModerate; limited by the inverter\nHow long it runs\tAs long as you have fuel\tUntil the battery empties; solar refills it by day\nNoise\tNoticeable engine noise\tNear-silent (fan only)\nUse indoors\tNever (carbon monoxide)\tYes\nMaintenance\tOil changes, fuel stabilizer, regular test runs\tKeep it charged\nReady after months in storage\tOnly if fuel was stabilized or drained\tYes, if topped up every few months\nSafety: the difference that matters most\n\nEvery gas generator produces carbon monoxide (CO), a gas you can’t see or smell. It is one of the leading causes of preventable deaths after storms. U.S. safety agencies are unambiguous:\n\nNever run a generator inside your home, garage, basement, crawlspace or shed, even with doors and windows open.\nRun it outdoors, more than 20 feet from windows, doors and vents, with the exhaust pointed away from the house.\nInstall battery-operated CO alarms, and turn the generator off and let it cool before refueling.\n\nA note from a physician: early carbon monoxide poisoning often feels like the flu: headache, dizziness, weakness, nausea or confusion. If several people or pets in the home feel sick at the same time while a generator is running, get everyone into fresh air immediately and call 911.\n\nA solar generator produces no exhaust, so it can sit next to your bed powering a CPAP or in your kitchen running the fridge. For apartments, condos and anyone without a safe outdoor spot 20 feet from the house, this alone often decides the question.\n\nCost example: a 3-day power outage\n\nLet’s cover the same essentials from our sizing guide: a refrigerator, Wi-Fi, lights, phones and a laptop, about 2.3 kWh per day.\n\nGas generator\nEquipment: a portable inverter generator of about 2,000–3,500 W.\nFuel (assumption): a small inverter generator at light load often burns roughly 0.1–0.2 gallons per hour. Running 12 hours a day for 3 days at 0.15 gal/h uses about 5.4 gallons, around $20 at $3.50 per gallon.\nThe catch: you need to store that fuel safely beforehand, because gas stations often can’t pump during widespread outages. You also can’t run it overnight near bedrooms without noise and CO precautions.\nSolar generator\nEquipment: a 2,000–3,000 Wh power station plus about 400–800 W of solar panels.\nRecharge (assumption): 400 W of panels with about 5 hours of good sun and typical real-world losses can put roughly 1.5 kWh per day back into the battery. 800 W roughly doubles that.\nThe catch: on cloudy days, solar recharge can drop sharply. Cycling the refrigerator and skipping the laptop helps you stretch the stored energy.\n\nThe takeaway: over one outage, gas is cheaper to run. Over years, the solar generator’s near-zero running cost, zero maintenance and indoor safety close much of the gap, and it doubles as an everyday tool for camping, road trips and backup for your home office.\n\nWhen a gas generator is the better choice\nYou regularly face outages longer than 3–4 days.\nYou need to run large loads: a well pump, a sump pump during heavy rain, a window air conditioner or power tools.\nYou have a safe outdoor location at least 20 feet from the house and you’re comfortable storing fuel and doing basic engine maintenance.\nWhen a solar generator is the better choice\nYou live in an apartment, condo or anywhere a gas generator isn’t practical or allowed.\nYour priorities are a refrigerator, a CPAP, Wi-Fi, lights and phones.\nYou want something that works silently at night and needs no maintenance between storms.\nYou’ll also use it for camping, tailgating or van life.\nThe hybrid option many people choose\n\nYou don’t have to pick one. A common setup is a power station indoors for silent overnight power, recharged during the day by a small gas generator running safely outside, or by solar panels when the sun cooperates. You get indoor safety at night and nearly unlimited runtime during long outages.\n\nFrequently asked questions\nCan I run a gas generator in my garage with the door open?\n\nNo. U.S. safety agencies warn that carbon monoxide can build to deadly levels in a garage even with the door open. Run it outside only, more than 20 feet from any door, window or vent.\n\nCan a solar generator power a whole house?\nIs a solar generator worth it if I live somewhere cloudy?\nThe bottom line\n\nChoose gas for long outages and heavy loads, if you can run it safely outdoors. Choose solar for silent, indoor-safe power for the essentials, with no fuel to store. If you can, combine them. Either way, never run a gas generator indoors. Not sure you need backup power at all? Read Is a portable power station worth it?, based on official U.S. outage data.\n\nSources\nCenters for Disease Control and Prevention, Carbon Monoxide Poisoning Basics.\nU.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission, Portable Generator Safety Alert (PDF).\nShare this:\n X Facebook\nLike this:\nLoading…\nAR\n\nAlejandro Rodríguez, MD\n\nPhysician (MD, Colombia)\n\nEvery Tested Watts guide is built from official specs, with the formula shown so you can check the math. Alejandro takes extra care with CPAP, cold, heat and food safety. He isn’t licensed to practice medicine in the U.S., and nothing here is medical advice.\n\nAbout\nHow we research\nReport an error\n\nGet the free Power Outage Runtime Chart\n\nOne printable page: how long a power station runs 13 common devices at 5 battery sizes. Then a short email before the big sales. No spam; unsubscribe anytime.\n\nType your email…\n\nEmail me the chart\n\nHave a question, or spotted an error? Contact us. We may earn a commission from qualifying purchases; see our Affiliate Disclosure.\n\nTested Watts\n\nSpec-based guides to portable power stations and solar generators. Every runtime shows its formula; every spec links to its source.\n\nStart here\n\nPower station calculator\nWhat size do I need?\nFridge runtime chart\nCPAP nights chart\n\nPopular\n\nBest for home backup\nEcoFlow vs Jackery\nBest for camping\nBest solar panels\n\nAbout\n\nAbout\nHow We Research\nEditorial Policy\nCorrections Policy\nContact\nAffiliate Disclosure\nPrivacy Policy\n\nAs an affiliate, Tested Watts may earn a commission from qualifying purchases made through links on this site, at no extra cost to you.\n\n© 2026 Tested Watts. All rights reserved.\n\nImpact-Site-Verification: af27a35e-3428-4dd3-b93c-b95bb5fd6d65",
